explanation:
The internal rate of return (IRR) is defined as the interest rate that makes the net present value (NPV) of all cash flows equal to zero, which can be expressed mathematically as: 
\begin{equation} \text{NPV} = \frac{C_0}{(1 + r)^0} + \frac{C_1}{(1 + r)^1} + \frac{C_2}{(1 + r)^2} + \text{...} + \frac{C_n}{(1 + r)^n} = 0 \\ \text{where } C_i \text{ are the cash flows and } r \text{ is the IRR.} 
\text{To find IRR, solve for } r \text{ such that NPV = 0.} 
\end{equation}
